Sauron turns "the Ring tempts you" into a free wheel: discard your hand, draw four. In almost any other deck that is a cost. Here it is the point — the cards you throw away are seven-drops you were never going to cast, and the graveyard is where this deck keeps its best creatures. Every temptation is card advantage AND a loaded graveyard at the same time.

# The commander
Sauron, the Dark Lord :: a 7/6 with a ward that eats a legendary permanent, an Orc Army that grows every time an opponent casts anything, and — the important line — discard your hand and draw four whenever the Ring tempts you.
Call of the Ring :: tempts you at the beginning of EVERY upkeep. With Sauron out that is a free wheel each turn, and it draws an extra card when you name a Ring-bearer.
Ringsight :: tempts you and tutors any card sharing a colour with a legend you control.
Sauron's Ransom :: tempts you, and either pile is fine — cards in hand are cards, cards in the graveyard are reanimation targets.
Orcish Bowmasters :: amasses alongside Sauron's own Orcs, and punishes every extra card the table draws.
The Army is a real clock. Once it connects, Sauron tempts you again, which wheels you again.

# Filling the graveyard on purpose
Entomb :: one mana, instant speed, exactly the creature you want.
Buried Alive :: three at once.
Careful Study :: two down for one mana.
Faithless Looting :: the same, twice with flashback.
Frantic Search :: free, and it untaps three lands.
Ledger Shredder :: connives on everyone's second spell each turn, filtering constantly.
Grave Researcher :: surveils every upkeep and prepares a free Reanimate once three creatures are down there.
Archfiend of Ifnir :: every discard puts a -1/-1 counter on each of their creatures. Discarding a full hand to Sauron's trigger is a one-sided board wipe.
Monument to Endurance :: every discard becomes a card, a Treasure or three life from each opponent. On a wheel turn it is all three.

# Bringing them back
Reanimate :: one mana, any graveyard.
Animate Dead :: two, and it works on theirs.
Dance of the Dead :: the third copy, and the untap clause rarely matters.
Necromancy :: flash speed, so you can reanimate at the end of their turn or in response to a wrath.
Persist :: two mana for any nonlegendary creature.
Chainer, Nightmare Adept :: discard a card to cast a creature from your graveyard each turn, and anything not cast from hand gets haste.
Living Death :: your graveyard is deliberately the deepest at the table.
Ancient Brass Dragon :: on connection, reanimate creatures with total mana value up to a d20 roll, from ANY graveyard.
Anger :: sitting in the graveyard with a Mountain out, everything you reanimate has haste.

# What you are reanimating
Archon of Cruelty :: the best target. On entry and on every attack, an opponent sacrifices, discards and loses three while you draw and gain three.
Burning-Rune Demon :: a two-card tutor where the "wrong" one goes to your graveyard, which is exactly where you want it.
Marang River Regent :: a 6/7 flier that bounces two of their permanents, and an Omen half that draws three when you would rather have cards.
Smaug, Wicked Worm :: Treasures scaled to their artifact count, and a card every time you spend Treasure mana.
Archfiend of Ifnir :: a 5/4 flier that turns your own wheels into sweepers.

# Interaction
Mana Drain :: counter, and the mana casts a reanimation spell.
Counterspell :: the clean two-mana answer.
Arcane Denial :: cheap, and the cards they get arrive too late.
An Offer You Can't Refuse :: one mana for anything noncreature.
Deadly Rollick :: free with your commander out.
Deflecting Swat :: free, and it redirects the removal aimed at Sauron.
Bitter Triumph :: a creature or planeswalker, and the discard cost is upside here.
Feed the Swarm :: the only clean enchantment answer in Grixis.
Chaos Warp :: the second one, and it covers planeswalkers.
Blasphemous Act :: cheap, and your best creatures are in the graveyard where a wrath cannot reach them.
Cyclonic Rift :: overloaded, the clear for a lethal reanimated board.
Dauthi Voidwalker :: unblockable, blanks their graveyards, and steals their best card.

# Closing the game
Chandra's Ignition :: point it at Archon of Cruelty or Sauron. Six or seven damage to every other creature on the board and to each opponent, all at once.
Shadowspear :: trample and lifelink on a fatty, and it strips indestructible and hexproof from their blockers.
Lightning Greaves :: haste for the thing you just reanimated, and shroud on Sauron.
Living Death :: after a couple of wheels, this is usually lethal on the spot.

# Card flow and mana
Rhystic Study :: their turns pay for yours.
Mystic Remora :: the best card in the deck on turns one through four.
Black Market Connections :: three effects a turn, and this deck's life total is not its bottleneck.
Relic of Sauron :: two coloured mana and a repeatable loot.
Fellwar Stone :: usually taps for exactly the pip you need.
Arcane Signet :: the same.

# What to keep in an opening hand
Keep an enabler and a reanimation spell. Entomb plus Reanimate with two lands beats any hand with an Archon of Cruelty in it, because the Archon is better in your graveyard than in your hand.
Do not be precious about your hand. Sauron's wheel discards everything — so cast what matters first, then take the temptation. Cards you were holding "just in case" become Reanimate targets.
Call of the Ring :: two mana, and with Sauron out it is a wheel every single upkeep. Prioritise it over almost any single fatty.
Sauron, the Dark Lord :: his ward costs you a legendary permanent, which is real. Lightning Greaves is often the better protection, because it means they cannot target him at all.
